{"subjectId":"python","id":"functions","title":"Functions & Arguments","group":"Functions","order":10,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Functions are first-class objects: you can pass them around, return them, and store them — and Python's argument system is unusually flexible.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Defining &amp; returning</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def area(w: float, h: float = 1.0) -&gt; float:\n    \"\"\"Return the area of a w × h rectangle.\"\"\"\n    return w * h\n\ndef min_max(xs):\n    return min(xs), max(xs)          # \"multiple\" return = one tuple\n\nlo, hi = min_max([4, 1, 9])\nprint(area(3, 2), area(5), lo, hi)   # 6 5.0 1 9\nprint(area.__doc__)                  # Return the area of a w × h rectangle.</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>A function with no <code>return</code> (or a bare <code>return</code>) returns <code>None</code>. Type hints are documentation — Python does not enforce them at runtime.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Kinds of parameters</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Syntax</th><th>Call as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positional or keyword</td><td><code>def f(a, b)</code></td><td><code>f(1, 2)</code> or <code>f(b=2, a=1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Default value</td><td><code>def f(a, b=10)</code></td><td><code>f(1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Variadic positional</td><td><code>def f(*args)</code></td><td><code>f(1, 2, 3)</code> → <code>args == (1, 2, 3)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Variadic keyword</td><td><code>def f(**kwargs)</code></td><td><code>f(x=1)</code> → <code>kwargs == {'x': 1}</code></td></tr><tr><td>Keyword-only</td><td><code>def f(a, *, key)</code></td><td><code>f(1, key=2)</code> — <code>key</code> must be named</td></tr><tr><td>Positional-only</td><td><code>def f(a, /, b)</code></td><td><code>f(1, 2)</code> — <code>a</code> can't be named</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def report(title, *items, sep=\", \", **meta):\n    line = f\"{title}: {sep.join(items)}\"\n    if meta:\n        line += f\" {meta}\"\n    return line\n\nprint(report(\"fruits\", \"apple\", \"kiwi\"))             # fruits: apple, kiwi\nprint(report(\"ids\", \"1\", \"2\", sep=\"|\", source=\"db\"))  # ids: 1|2 {'source': 'db'}\n\nopts = {\"sep\": \"-\"}\nprint(report(\"unpacked\", *[\"a\", \"b\"], **opts))       # unpacked: a-b</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>The mutable default trap</h3>\n<p>Default values are evaluated <strong>once</strong>, when <code>def</code> runs — not on each call. A mutable default is shared by every call.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def add_bad(item, bucket=[]):\n    bucket.append(item)\n    return bucket\n\nprint(add_bad(1), add_bad(2))     # [1, 2] [1, 2]\n\ndef add_good(item, bucket=None):\n    if bucket is None:\n        bucket = []\n    bucket.append(item)\n    return bucket\n\nprint(add_good(1), add_good(2))   # [1] [2]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Rule</div><p>Use <code>None</code> as the default for lists, dicts and sets, and create the object inside the function.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>How arguments are passed</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Pass by object reference</strong> — the function receives a reference to the <em>same object</em>. Mutating it is visible to the caller; rebinding the parameter name is not.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def mutate(xs):\n    xs.append(99)      # changes the caller's list\n\ndef rebind(xs):\n    xs = [0]           # only changes the local name\n\ndata = [1]\nmutate(data); rebind(data)\nprint(data)            # [1, 99]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-refresh\"></i>Recursion</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import sys\n\ndef fact(n: int) -&gt; int:\n    return 1 if n &lt;= 1 else n * fact(n - 1)\n\nprint(fact(10), sys.getrecursionlimit())   # 3628800 1000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>CPython has no tail-call optimisation and a default recursion limit of ~1000 frames. For deep recursion (DFS on 10⁵ nodes) use an explicit stack, or raise the limit with <code>sys.setrecursionlimit</code>.</p></div></section>"}